      "writtenOn": "2026-06-22T16:00:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why `u64`?  Can this be `u32`?  [The spec](https://www.w3.org/TR/png-3/#5Chunk-layout) uses 4 bytes for chunk length (and even augments this further by saying \"Although encoders and decoders should treat the length as unsigned, its value shall not exceed 2^31-1 bytes.\")",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 435,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 435,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "929fc0961e714fe80b0e2b3a3c4a6f25d50ce7eb",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "baab6245_3914cc85",
        "filename": "rust/png/FFI.rs",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 435,
      "author": {
        "id": 4772438
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-22T16:40:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess I did that because:\n\n1) The `length + 4` operation could potentially overflow\n2) `Take::set_limit` takes a `u64` as an input, so exposing that felt more natural\n\nFor 1), while the spec mentions that the length \"shall not exceed 2^31-1 bytes\", I wonder if a malformed PNG could potentially contain a `u32::MAX` length? \n\nWhile a malformed PNG should be caught by the decoder, considering this state machine is sitting in the middle, I wonder if that could create potential issues?",
      "parentUuid": "b3e930d0_564b2862",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 435,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 435,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "929fc0961e714fe80b0e2b3a3c4a6f25d50ce7eb",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2076b169_df4a25b4",
        "filename": "rust/png/FFI.rs",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 435,
      "author": {
        "id": 4772438
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-22T16:40:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I used `u64` here for a couple of reasons:\n\n1) **Overflow safety with `length + 4`:** While the PNG spec says chunk length \"shall not exceed 2^31-1 bytes\", a malformed/malicious PNG could contain a `u32::MAX` length.\n2) **API Alignment:** `Take::set_limit` takes a `u64`, so using `u64` felt more natural.\n\nMore importantly, if we were to use `u32` with `saturating_add(4)`, a malformed PNG with `length \u003d u32::MAX` would cause `remaining` to saturate to `u32::MAX`. This would cause our state machine (`PngStateMachine`) to desynchronize from the actual stream:\n- It would think the chunk is finished after reading `u32::MAX` bytes (believing it read the CRC too, which it hasn\u0027t).\n- It would then prematurely transition to `ChunkHeader` and interpret the actual 4-byte CRC of the giant chunk as the start of the next chunk\u0027s header.\n- This desynchronization would corrupt the limits for all subsequent chunks, potentially causing premature EOFs or failing to limit them.\n\nBy keeping `remaining` as `u64`, we can represent `length + 4` exactly even for `u32::MAX`, ensuring the state machine stays in sync and the decoder fails safely with an unexpected EOF when it tries to read the missing CRC.",
      "revId": "929fc0961e714fe80b0e2b3a3c4a6f25d50ce7eb",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
